  "grammar": {
    "md": "Xenophon writes plain, clear Attic; the *Memorabilia* ('Recollections') is his defence of Socrates. This lesson reads him for two things: the **questions Socrates asked**, and the **way he asked them**.\n\n**What is X?** Xenophon lists Socrates' questions as a string of τί + neuter adjective: τί **εὐσεβές**, τί **ἀσεβές**, τί **καλόν**, τί **αἰσχρόν**, τί **δίκαιον**, τί **ἄδικον**. The neuter adjective names the quality (τὸ δίκαιον 'the just'); abstract nouns do the same work: ἡ **σωφροσύνη**, ἡ **ἀνδρεία**, ἡ **σοφία**. The opposites come in pairs made with the negative prefix ἀ- / ἀν-: δίκαιος ↔ ἄδικος, εὐσεβής ↔ ἀσεβής, ὅσιος ↔ ἀνόσιος, γιγνώσκω ↔ ἀγνοέω.\n\n**Which of the two?** Socrates offers a choice and makes the learner pick:\n\n| Greek | meaning |\n|---|---|\n| **πότερον** δίκαιόν ἐστιν **ἢ** ἄδικον; | is it just or unjust? |\n| δίκαια ποιεῖ **ἢ** ἄδικα; | (the second question needs no πότερον) |\n| **τί δέ;** | well then? what about this? |\n| ἐπισκεψώμεθα | let us examine (hortatory subjunctive) |\n\nThe trick is the next question: the general who deceives the enemy lies, yet does what is just. The learner's easy answer collapses, and he is at a loss (ἠπόρει) or silent (ἐσίγα). This is the ἔλεγχος you met in Lesson 1.\n\n**Participles that carry the sense.** πληθούσης τῆς ἀγορᾶς (genitive absolute) 'when the market was full'; ἐφαίνετο ἀγνοῶν 'he was plainly ignorant' (φαίνομαι + participle: clearly is); οἱ ἑαυτοὺς γιγνώσκοντες 'those who know themselves'.",
    "paradigms": [
      "dikaios",
      "alethes"
    ],
    "diagram": "diagram-participle-map"
  },
