  "culture": {
    "title": "Xenophon remembers Socrates",
    "md": "**Xenophon** son of Gryllus (c. 430 – c. 355 BC) was born in the deme Erchia, a horseman from a well-off family. As a young man he spent time with Socrates; in 401 he left Athens to serve with Cyrus in Persia (the *Anabasis*), so he was not in the city when Socrates was tried in 399. Exiled soon after, he lived for years on an estate at Scillus near Olympia given him by the Spartans, and there, and later in Corinth, he wrote.\n\nThe **Memorabilia** (Ἀπομνημονεύματα, 'Recollections') begins as an answer to the charges: Socrates was not impious, since he sacrificed openly and never spoke of the heavens like the natural philosophers; he did not corrupt the young, since he made them better. Most of the work is short conversations: with his son about his mother's temper, with an armourer about fit, with the young **Euthydemus**, who owned the finest library of poets and sophists and thought himself wise.\n\nXenophon's Socrates is more practical than Plato's: he gives advice about health, money and friendship as well as asking τί ἐστι; But the method is the same: a question, an easy answer, a counter-example, and ἀπορία. The Delphic maxim **γνῶθι σαυτόν**, 'know yourself', was inscribed at Apollo's temple at Delphi; a Roman mosaic of a reclining skeleton with these words is now in the Museo Nazionale Romano.",
    "image": "phil-culture-3-gnothi-mosaic"
  },
